<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Finland</h1><p class="st-meta">1556 CE – 1563 CE · capital Turku</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Short-lived Finnish duchy granted to the future John III of Sweden, whose independent policy led his brother Eric XIV to crush it in 1563.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1556 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-i-of-sweden">Gustav Vasa</a> granted his second son John the Duchy of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-finland">Finland</a>, covering south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a>. John set up his court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turku-castle">Turku Castle</a>, which he enlarged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> style, and governed with considerable independence.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Duke John ran his own administration, collected taxes and pursued a foreign policy of his own, which brought him into conflict with his half-brother, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-xiv-of-sweden">Eric XIV</a>. In 1562 he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-jagiellon">Catherine Jagiellon</a>, sister of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and lent money to Poland while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and Poland competed for <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-confederation">Livonia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-xiv-of-sweden">Eric XIV</a> regarded this as treason. In 1563 his army besieged and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turku-castle">Turku Castle</a>, and John and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-jagiellon">Catherine</a> were imprisoned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gripsholm-castle">Gripsholm</a>. The duchy was abolished, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-finland">Finland</a> returned to direct rule by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden</a>. John deposed Eric in 1568 and reigned as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-of-sweden">John III</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="duchy, 1556 CE – 1563 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">duchy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:90.67%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1556 CE – 1563 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Turku</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swedish, Finnish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/finland">Finland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Finland (duchy), Hertigdömet Finland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1560&amp;focus=duchy-of-finland" aria-label="Duchy of Finland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5144 156 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Finland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5464 412L5381 440"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5381 440h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5464 412h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5464 412h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Turku. <a href="/atlas?year=1560&amp;focus=duchy-of-finland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

